  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent analysis shows that the primary axis of the 500mb ridge is \ndirectly over MO while the primary trough axis extends from WA \ntowards southern NV. At the surface, the broad area of high pressure \nis centered over Ohio and the Appalachian Mountains while low \npressure continues to build in over the Intermountain West. This \nsetup is contributing to the increasing pressure gradient seen over \nthe Great Plains and is contributing to the breezy and gusty \nconditions present for the NW half of our CWA. A large swath of \nmoisture transport near the surface is supporting a dense layer of \nstratus over KS but that should remain west of our area until the \nearly morning hours. By Wednesday morning, the 500mb trough will \nlift NE across the Northern Great Plains supporting lee cyclogenesis \noff of the Rockies while high pressure over the Appalachians will \nremain mostly stationary, further tightening the pressure gradient \nover our area. As a result we will see sustained winds around 15-20 \nmph and gusts around 25-35 mph, especially for eastern KS and NW MO. \nLow-level moisture transport will increase into the area tomorrow \nmorning which will support some morning clouds that will clear out \nby the afternoon.\n\nBy Thursday, the 500mb trough will lift NE out of the Northern \nPlains and broad mid-level ridging will again dominate the SE \nquadrant of the CONUS, including the Midwest. Southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow in addition to ongoing WAA advection will result \nin rising temperatures through the end of the work week with highs \nincreasing into the upper 60s/low 70s by Friday. By this weekend \nanother 500mb trough will make its way east, supporting a developing \nsurface low over the Northern Plains with a trailing cold front. \nThis front, along with the moist southerly flow already advecting \ninto the region, will support increased rain chances (30-60%) for \nthis weekend. Looking at ensemble guidance, shear values still \nremain high but CAPE continues to trend downward so the potential \nfor more organized convection looks low at this time. The exact \ntiming of this system still remains somewhat uncertain as the models \nkeep pushing back the timing of the cold front and associated rain, \nso we will continue to monitor model trends. However, once this cold \nfront moves through the region we will see a return to more \nseasonable temperatures with highs in the mid to upper 40s and lows \nin the upper 20s to low 30s.",
